# Prisma records a SHA-256 of each migration file in `_prisma_migrations` when
# it applies it, and re-checks that hash on every `migrate dev`. A checksum is
# taken over raw bytes, so a line ending is a content change as far as it is
# concerned.
#
# This machine has `core.autocrlf=true`. Without the rule below, migrations
# written on the Mac (LF, hashed as LF) get checked out here as CRLF, and Prisma
# reports five "modified after it was applied" migrations and offers to reset
# the database — which on a database holding real work is the most destructive
# button in the toolchain, offered for a bug that is entirely cosmetic. Found
# 2026-08-05, when four of the five 2026-08-01 migrations mismatched purely on
# line endings and normalising them back to LF made all four match byte for byte.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Same argument, one step earlier: the seed and the schema are read by tooling
# that hashes or diffs them, and a repo that flips every line on checkout makes
# every diff between the two machines unreadable.
*.prisma text eol=lf
